# queue_and_tree as FOUR cells (cell 0 class Queue, 1 record node, build, count, 2 walk, 3 main), Unicon entry, developer_15 2026-11-22. THE STEP SCRIPT RUNS EVERY EARLIER CELL FIRST (cells 0, 1, 2, no stop), then the breaks and cell 3.
# THE LAW OF EACH STEP, worked out from the program and unicon 13.3's image() before any run: main's lines in cell 3 are 3 t := build, 4 c := count(t), 5 write nodes, 6 s := walk(t); walk's in cell 2: 3 q := Queue(), 4 q.push(root); a procedure's FIRST STOP is its first executable statement (a local declaration is no statement), so a step in at line 6 enters walk at line 3;
# the break at cell 2 line 4 stops there with q the object Queue_1(1); a step in at q.push(root) enters Queue_push, which lives in CELL 0 at line 3 (put (items, item)), frames Queue_push, walk, main, item the root record node_1(2).
